CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a four-column visualization of the activations inside a small neural network, the columns arranged left to right as successive stages of training. Each column is a vertical stack of seven flat square planes in isometric perspective, one plane per layer output, joined by dashed trajectory curves that carry every datapoint from one plane to the next. Points appear in two colors, red and blue. In all four columns the bottom plane shows the two colors thoroughly intermingled in a dense central cluster, and moving upward they are pulled apart, the red trajectories drawing into a tight vertical bundle while the blue ones bow outward to one side. A legend column at the far left gives the architecture as a bottom-to-top chain of arrows and rounded labels reading linear, relu, linear, relu, linear, and softmax. Beneath the panels a long horizontal arrow labeled Training iteration runs the full width, with sets of three dots between columns marking skipped iterations. Reading up a column shows how one forward pass reshapes the data distribution layer by layer; reading across shows the separation growing cleaner and the blue points swinging farther out as training proceeds.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - vertical stack of 7 flat square planes (layer-activation slices) drawn as isometric diamonds, evenly spaced in height
  - faint grid lines subdividing each square plane into a light 4x4 mesh
  - dark gray outline border around each square plane
  - thin gray dashed lines joining the four corners of each plane to the corners of the plane above (the 'box' scaffolding between layers)
  - red datapoints (class 0) scattered on every plane, dense and intermingled on the bottom plane, pulled into a tight vertical bundle near plane center toward the top
  - blue datapoints (class 1) scattered on every plane, intermingled at the bottom, bowing outward to one side and off-plane toward the top
  - dashed red trajectory curves connecting each red point from its position on one plane to its position on the next plane above
  - dashed blue trajectory curves connecting each blue point through the same stack
  - top (softmax) plane where red points collapse to one corner region and blue points to another
  - far-left legend column: vertical chain of upward arrows alternating with rounded-rectangle labels reading, bottom to top, linear, relu, linear, relu, linear, softmax
  - bottom horizontal arrow spanning the full figure width labeled 'Training iteration'
  - ellipsis (three dots) marks along the training axis indicating skipped iterations
  - small tick markers on the training axis at the four sampled iterations

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
One column only is shown at a time (the slider replaces the four side-by-side panels). Planes: 7 squares, each 2.0 x 2.0 world units lying in the XZ plane (+X screen-right, +Z receding diagonal), centered on the Y axis, stacked at y = 0, 1.6, 3.2, 4.8, 6.4, 8.0, 9.6 (total stack height 9.6, roughly 5x its width, matching the tall thin columns in the source). Plane order bottom→top: input, after linear1, after relu1, after linear2, after relu2, after linear3, after softmax; the six labeled transitions in the legend sit between consecutive planes. Each plane has a dark-gray border (#444444, ~1.5px) and a faint 4x4 internal grid (#cccccc). Gray dashed corner-to-corner scaffolding lines (#999999, dash 0.06) connect matching corners of adjacent planes. ~70 datapoints, ~35 red and ~35 blue, sphere/point radius ~0.035 world units. Red #ee1111, blue #1111ee, both drawn with ~0.7 opacity; trajectory curves are dashed, same hue as their point, thin (~1px), dash length ~0.05. Layout of the data by height and training progress t in [0,1]: bottom plane always a dense isotropic cluster of radius ~0.35 centered at the plane origin with red and blue fully interleaved (identical at all t). Moving upward, red points contract toward the column axis — lateral spread shrinking from 0.35 at the bottom to ~0.08 at the top when t=1, only to ~0.25 when t=0 — forming the tight vertical red bundle. Blue points migrate monotonically in +X (screen-right) as height increases; peak lateral offset of the blue cloud at the upper planes is ~0.3 units at t=0, ~0.9 at t=0.33, ~1.6 at t=0.66, and ~2.6 at t=1, so at late training the blue trajectories overshoot the plane edge and fan out to the right of the stack (exactly as in the rightmost source column). Relu planes additionally clamp roughly a third of the points onto the plane's axis lines (a visible line/corner concentration). On the top softmax plane red collapses to a small cluster at the left/center and blue to a cluster at the right corner. Legend column drawn to the left of the stack at x ~ -3.2, spanning the same y range: six rounded-rectangle labels (white fill, dark gray 1px stroke, monospace text) reading bottom→top linear, relu, linear, relu, linear, softmax, each preceded by a short upward arrow, vertically aligned with the gap between the corresponding pair of planes. Beneath everything at y ~ -1.2 a horizontal arrow spanning the figure width labeled 'Training iteration' (serif text) with four tick marks and two sets of ellipsis dots between them; the slider handle rides along this axis.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "trainingIteration",
    "type": "slider",
    "teaches": "as optimization proceeds the same input cloud becomes progressively more separable: red trajectories tighten into a vertical bundle while blue ones swing farther outward, so class separability is a product of training progress, not architecture alone",
    "label": "Training iteration",
    "range": [
      0,
      100,
      1
    ],
    "default": 0
  },
  {
    "id": "layerStep",
    "type": "state",
    "teaches": "each layer performs one concrete reshaping of the same point set — linear layers rotate/stretch, relu folds points against the origin, softmax collapses them onto the class simplex",
    "label": "Forward pass",
    "states": [
      "input",
      "linear",
      "relu",
      "linear2",
      "relu2",
      "linear3",
      "softmax",
      "all"
    ],
    "default": "all"
  },
  {
    "id": "hoverTrajectory",
    "type": "hover",
    "teaches": "a single datapoint keeps its identity through every layer; brightening its full path shows the continuity between layerwise transformations and the final class bundle"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitStack",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "the trajectories live in 3D between the planes — rotating reveals that the blue bow-out is a real lateral displacement, not an artifact of the isometric drawing"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Untrained network",
    "narration": "At the first iteration red and blue points stay intermingled all the way up the stack; even the softmax plane shows the two colors overlapping near the center.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 0,
      "layerStep": "all"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Bottom plane: the raw input",
    "narration": "The lowest plane is the untransformed data — a dense central cluster with red and blue thoroughly mixed. No layer has acted yet.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 0,
      "layerStep": "input"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "First linear then relu",
    "narration": "Step up one plane at a time: the linear layer shears and spreads the cloud, then relu folds part of it, beginning to push blue points off to one side.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 33,
      "layerStep": "relu"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Mid-training, deeper layers",
    "narration": "Partway through training the upper linear layers already draw red into a narrow vertical bundle while blue trajectories bow outward well beyond the plane edge.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 66,
      "layerStep": "linear3"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Converged softmax",
    "narration": "Late in training the softmax plane holds two tight, widely separated bundles — reading up the column shows how each layer contributed to that separation.",
    "state": {
      "trainingIteration": 100,
      "layerStep": "softmax"
    }
  }
]

AXIS ANGLES: null — null means the source is a flat 2D figure; pass axisScreenAngles: null to setCameraView for a front-on view.

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.72
}

NOTES: Render with an orthographic camera in a standard isometric pose so the square planes read as diamonds. Planes: THREE.PlaneGeometry rotated -90deg about X, with a separate LineSegments grid and an EdgesGeometry border; keep them depthWrite:false with low-opacity fill so trajectories behind remain visible. Trajectories: one CatmullRomCurve3 per datapoint sampled through its 7 plane positions, converted to a LineSegments dashed line (LineDashedMaterial + computeLineDistances) or Line2/LineMaterial for consistent pixel width. Precompute 4 keyframe layouts (the four source columns) and lerp per-point positions with the slider, recomputing curve geometry each frame — cheap at ~70 curves x 7 control points. Datapoints as a single THREE.Points cloud or InstancedMesh of small spheres, per-instance color. Hover: raycast against an invisible thickened tube/line-picking proxy per trajectory (or nearest-point-in-screen-space search over projected control points); on hit, raise that curve's opacity to 1.0, widen it, and dim all others to ~0.08. Layer stepping: highlight the active plane (stronger border, slight emissive fill) and show only the trajectory segments up to and including that transition, fading the rest; 'all' shows the complete stack. Legend labels, the 'Training iteration' arrow label and ellipses are best done as CSS2DRenderer HTML overlays for crisp monospace/serif type. Render order: dashed gray scaffolding first, then trajectories, then planes with transparency, then points on top.

Follow the interaction plan above.

